    'Guillain–Barré syndrome is an acute immune-mediated polyradiculoneuropathy, often after Campylobacter jejuni, cytomegalovirus, Epstein–Barr virus, Zika or vaccination, presenting with progressive ascending weakness, areflexia, variable sensory symptoms, cranial nerve involvement, autonomic instability and pain, with albuminocytological dissociation in the cerebrospinal fluid and demyelinating changes on nerve conduction (axonal variants and Miller Fisher syndrome with anti-GQ1b antibodies also occur). Treatment is intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange, which are equally effective and not combined; corticosteroids are ineffective. The priority in management is monitoring and supporting respiratory and bulbar function — serial vital capacity and negative inspiratory force, with elective intubation before crisis — plus cardiac monitoring for arrhythmia and blood pressure swings, thromboprophylaxis, pain control, bladder and bowel care, nutrition, pressure area care and early rehabilitation.',
